{
  "interpretation": "The patch refactors app/db.py to replace full-table scans with parameterized SQL queries, achieving a significant latency reduction. The candidate median (0.58 ms) is approximately 6.8x faster than the parent (4.09 ms), yielding a relative improvement of 0.84. All 6000 operations succeeded with zero semantic mismatches, confirming API contract preservation. Validation passed. The implementation uses SQLite's json_each for tag filtering and LIKE for text search, adhering to the plan's hypothesis of reducing O(N) complexity. Noise levels are low (CV 0.019).",
  "limitations": [
    "Performance gains are measured on a small dataset; scalability benefits for large tables are unverified.",
    "SQLite JSON functions require version 3.38+; compatibility with older environments is not confirmed.",
    "The 'total' count query performs a full scan under filters, which may bottleneck on large datasets.",
    "Tag filtering logic relies on SQL subqueries which may have higher overhead than in-memory filtering for very small result sets."
  ],
  "schema_version": 1
}
